WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.339
Here's problem one.

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:09.230
Use the stationary principle to find q one, q two and q three, in terms of the spring stiffnesses and the loads.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.400
Let's read the figure slowly, piece by piece.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.405
On the left, a wall.

00:00:14.405 --> 00:00:20.630
Along the top, spring K one runs from the wall to this tall vertical bar.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:23.700
The bar moves to the right by q two.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.616
Along the bottom, spring K two runs from the wall to a junction that moves q one.

00:00:29.616 --> 00:00:33.118
Then a second spring runs from that junction to the bar.

00:00:33.118 --> 00:00:35.870
The figure doesn't label that second spring.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.560
We'll take it to be another K two, since the label sits right between them.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.750
If your instructor says otherwise, only that one stiffness changes.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:50.250
On the right, spring K three joins the bar to an outer point that moves q three.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:59.073
The loads: P one pulls the junction, P two and P three both push on the bar, and P four pulls the outer point.

00:00:59.073 --> 00:01:00.390
All to the right.

00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:04.981
One assumption about the bar: it's rigid and slides without tilting.

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:08.963
So every spring attached to it moves exactly q two at that end.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.245
Step one, the one that decides everything: the stretch of each spring.

00:01:15.245 --> 00:01:20.820
Remember, a stretch is the right end's displacement minus the left end's.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:31.244
K one: left end on the wall, which doesn't move, right end on the bar, which moves q two.

00:01:31.244 --> 00:01:32.490
Stretch: q two.

00:01:33.654 --> 00:01:36.989
The first K two: wall to the junction.

00:01:36.989 --> 00:01:38.305
Stretch: q one.

00:01:38.305 --> 00:01:41.288
The second K two: junction to bar.

00:01:41.288 --> 00:01:45.325
Its left end moves q one, its right end q two.

00:01:45.325 --> 00:01:47.694
Stretch: q two minus q one.

00:01:47.994 --> 00:01:52.443
K three: bar to the outer point.

00:01:52.443 --> 00:01:56.474
Stretch: q three minus q two.

00:01:57.622 --> 00:02:08.935
This is the step to get right, so let's say it once more: a spring between two moving points stretches by the difference of their displacements.

00:02:08.935 --> 00:02:12.942
Get these four right, and the rest is just algebra.

00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:31.233
Now the strain energy: one half, stiffness, times stretch squared, for each spring, all added up.

00:02:31.533 --> 00:02:53.813
U equals one half K one q two squared, plus one half K two q one squared, plus one half K two, q two minus q one, squared, plus one half K three, q three minus q two, squared.

00:02:54.113 --> 00:03:00.482
The work potential: each load times the displacement of the point it's on.

00:03:00.482 --> 00:03:02.203
P one acts at q one.

00:03:02.203 --> 00:03:06.334
P two and P three both act on the bar, at q two.

00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:08.313
P four acts at q three.

00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:10.750
And Pi is U minus W.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:19.443
Three unknowns, so we'll take three partial derivatives and set each one to zero.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.216
Start with q one.

00:03:23.216 --> 00:03:33.147
It appears in two springs: the first K two, and the second K two, where it shows up inside q two minus q one.

00:03:33.447 --> 00:03:35.656
The first gives K two q one.

00:03:35.656 --> 00:03:42.361
The second gives K two times q two minus q one, times minus one, from the chain rule.

00:03:42.361 --> 00:03:44.807
And the load gives minus P one.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:55.311
Multiply out: K two q one, minus K two q two, plus K two q one, minus P one, equals zero.

00:03:55.311 --> 00:04:00.683
The two K two q one terms make two K two q one.

00:04:00.683 --> 00:04:06.740
So: two K two q one, minus K two q two, equals P one.

00:04:07.074 --> 00:04:07.826
Now q two.

00:04:07.826 --> 00:04:11.289
It appears in three springs and one load term.

00:04:11.289 --> 00:04:16.634
Your turn first: pause and do checkpoint three point one in your guide.

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:21.724
K one gives K one q two.

00:04:21.724 --> 00:04:26.291
The second K two gives plus K two times q two minus q one.

00:04:26.291 --> 00:04:31.409
K three gives K three times q three minus q two, times minus one.

00:04:31.409 --> 00:04:34.874
And the loads give minus P two plus P three.

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:48.247
Multiply out and group by unknown: minus K two q one, plus K one plus K two plus K three, times q two, minus K three q three, equals P two plus P three.

00:04:48.574 --> 00:04:50.149
Last, q three.

00:04:50.149 --> 00:05:01.063
Only K three and P four contain it: K three times q three minus q two, minus P four, equals zero.

00:05:01.063 --> 00:05:07.814
So minus K three q two, plus K three q three, equals P four.

00:05:12.399 --> 00:05:15.519
Stack the three equations as a matrix.

00:05:15.852 --> 00:05:19.252
Check it against the pattern from last episode.

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:26.124
Each spring adds plus k on the diagonal of each free node it touches, and minus k between them.

00:05:26.124 --> 00:05:33.212
Springs tied to the wall only add to one diagonal, because the wall's row and column are left out.

00:05:33.497 --> 00:05:41.064
And it's symmetric: each spring puts the same minus k in both of its off-diagonal spots.

00:05:41.064 --> 00:05:42.697
Now let's solve it.

00:05:44.332 --> 00:05:53.927
Here's the plan: get rid of q three first, then q one, until one equation is left with q two alone.

00:05:53.927 --> 00:06:01.292
Look at the third equation: K three times q three minus q two equals P four.

00:06:01.592 --> 00:06:03.728
Add it to the second equation.

00:06:03.728 --> 00:06:13.552
The K three terms cancel, and we're left with minus K two q one, plus K one plus K two times q two, equals P two plus P three plus P four.

00:06:13.852 --> 00:06:15.741
That makes sense physically.

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:27.412
It's the force balance of the bar together with the outer point: the two springs on the bar's left side, K one and the second K two, hold P two, P three and P four together.

00:06:27.712 --> 00:06:34.112
From the first equation, q one equals P one plus K two q two, all over two K two.

00:06:34.412 --> 00:06:35.919
Substitute.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:46.332
Minus K two times q one is minus one half P one, minus one half K two q two.

00:06:46.665 --> 00:06:55.200
So the q two terms are K one plus K two, minus one half K two, which is K one plus one half K two.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:05.825
The equation becomes: K one plus one half K two, times q two, minus one half P one, equals P two plus P three plus P four.

00:07:06.199 --> 00:07:09.078
Multiply through by two and solve.

00:07:09.078 --> 00:07:17.799
q two equals P one, plus two times the sum of P two, P three and P four, all over two K one plus K two.

00:07:18.132 --> 00:07:31.092
Then q one is P one plus K two q two, over two K two, and q three is q two plus P four over K three.

00:07:32.831 --> 00:07:35.632
Never hand in a formula you haven't tested.

00:07:35.632 --> 00:07:41.754
Pick easy numbers: every spring one hundred newtons per millimeter, every load thirty newtons.

00:07:41.754 --> 00:07:45.271
Pause and do checkpoint three point two in your guide.

00:07:48.471 --> 00:07:56.111
q two is thirty plus two times ninety, which is two hundred ten, over three hundred: zero point seven millimeters.

00:07:56.380 --> 00:08:06.225
q one: P one plus K two q two is thirty plus seventy, one hundred, over two hundred: zero point five.

00:08:06.225 --> 00:08:13.340
q three is zero point seven plus thirty over one hundred: one millimeter.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:15.528
Now Newton, as a check.

00:08:15.528 --> 00:08:26.030
Spring K three holds only P four, so it should carry thirty newtons: one hundred times its stretch, zero point three, is thirty.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:26.440
Good.

00:08:26.774 --> 00:08:33.034
And the two springs on the wall must hold all four loads together, one hundred twenty newtons.

00:08:33.034 --> 00:08:36.564
K one carries seventy, the first K two carries fifty.

00:08:36.564 --> 00:08:39.294
Seventy plus fifty is one hundred twenty.

00:08:39.674 --> 00:08:48.702
So, stepping back: stretches, then U and W, then one derivative per unknown, then solve and check.

00:08:48.702 --> 00:08:50.912
That's the whole method.

00:08:50.912 --> 00:08:55.794
Next, we leave springs behind and look at a real bar.
